Subject: GTK on Phoenix: an opaque view's frames are drawn without alpha (opt-in)

The web process's dma-buf frames are AB24 (patch 0016's texture export: an RGBA8 texture), and
GTK 3 draws a GL texture with alpha by blending it over what lies below the view: in
gdk_cairo_draw_from_gl() (gdk/gdkgl.c, GTK 3.24.52) a texture whose GL_TEXTURE_ALPHA_SIZE is not 0
(GDK asks on GL ES 3.1 and later, gdkglcontext.c; before that it assumes alpha) makes GDK first
upload the window's cairo surface under the view as a texture of its own
(gdk_gl_texture_from_surface() of the clip region), then draw the frame with GL_BLEND. With
alpha 0 it draws the frame's quads opaque and only marks them flushed, so end_paint uploads
nothing there either. The paint watch (0105) measured ~7 ms per frame for that draw on the
Raspberry Pi 4 at alpha_bits=8.

An opaque view's frames have alpha 255 everywhere: the web process clears to transparent only
when the view's background colour is not opaque (AcceleratedSurface::clear() and isOpaque()),
otherwise the page is drawn over its opaque base background. Upstream uses that with GBM, where
the web process picks an opaque buffer format for an opaque view (SwapChain::setupBufferFormat());
our texture export has no such choice. With WEBKIT_PHOENIX_OPAQUE=1 (webkit-browser
--opaque-frames) the UI process does it on its side: while the view is opaque (no background
colour, or an opaque one -- webkit-browser sets none), each EGLImage buffer is imported as
XB24 instead of AB24 (AR24 -> XR24): the same memory, the alpha byte ignored. Mesa makes such a
dma-buf EGLImage a GL_RGB8 texture (dri_util.c: XBGR8888 -> GL_RGB8, so the alpha size is 0) and
the V3D samples it as RGBX8 (v3dx_format_table.c, alpha read as 1). The view's opacity is checked
when a frame becomes current, so a background colour set later re-imports the buffer with alpha
before it is drawn again; a format the EGL implementation refuses is logged once and not tried
again (the frame is then drawn with alpha, as before).

The paint watch's import line shows the format the texture was made with, once per format:

  WPEB-WEBKIT gtk-paint import pid=<p> size=<w>x<h> fourcc=XB24 modifier=0x... alpha_bits=0

and the frame-pacing line (0106) says whether a view uses it:

  WPEB-WEBKIT frame-pacing pid=<p> ahead=0|1 opaque=0|1

Off by default (an A/B of draw_ms in one image). What it cannot cover: a frame region the web
process does not draw (none expected over an opaque base background) shows its stale RGB instead
of the GTK background below the view.

